WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:04.520
Several places are still far far away

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:05.760
from ending their epidemics

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.120
and in fact are seeing their epidemics increase

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.460
and that's what I'm focusing on.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:14.500
Asking the question why and who's there

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:18.080
on the ground trying to fix things.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:24.920
My name is John Cohen.

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:27.519
I'm a staff writer with Science magazine.

00:00:27.519 --> 00:00:30.349
the Pulitzer Center funded travel for this project

00:00:30.349 --> 00:00:31.800
which also involved a collaboration

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:33.520
with the PBS Newshour.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:35.960
I’ve been covering the epidemic around the world now

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:37.969
for nearly 30 years.

00:00:37.969 --> 00:00:40.060
When I began doing this I’d go on trips

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.420
to other countries and see warehouses

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:43.650
full of people dying.

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:46.840
I don't see that now but I do see places that

00:00:46.859 --> 00:00:51.050
are struggling to use the very powerful tools that exist

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:54.219
both to help keep people alive and to stop them

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.040
from spreading the virus to other people.

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:00.360
About one out of four mother to child

00:01:00.379 --> 00:01:03.629
transmissions in the world happen in Nigeria.

00:01:03.629 --> 00:01:06.010
And Nigeria has had billions of dollars

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:08.100
of foreign aid poured into the country

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:09.540
to help solve the problem.

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:11.790
So what's wrong?

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:15.340
The big problem is so many pregnant women

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:17.720
never intersect with healthcare.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:20.650
They either give birth at home or they have a traditional

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:23.320
birth attendant but she's not necessarily

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:25.360
a trained health care professional

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:27.660
who's gonna do HIV testing.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:30.400
So one of the creative things that Nigeria has

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.520
going right now is a project working with

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:35.180
traditional birth attendants.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:37.080
I met with a traditional birth attendant

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:40.820
Mama Metta and they have trained Mama Metta

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:43.600
to encourage women to come to her clinic

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:47.140
on a day when an HIV testing group shows up

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.960
and she also knows that if a woman does test positive

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:51.920
that she should transfer her to a hospital or a

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:54.350
regular clinic for care.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:56.320
So that's one of the progressive things that's happening

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:59.520
in Nigeria to try to really isolate a problem.

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:03.340
About 90% of Nigerians go to services on the weekends

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:05.770
whether they're Christian or Muslim and the country's

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:08.580
about 50/50 so a project has begun

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:11.660
called baby shower where they go to the churches

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:14.050
and they have the priests say all pregnant women

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:15.390
And their partners come to the front for

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:16.540
a special blessing.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:18.680
And then the priest says

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:22.660
after the service we're gonna have health testing here

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:24.890
we're gonna check you for sickle-cell anemia

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:27.380
and we're gonna check you for hepatitis B

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:28.730
and we're gonna check you for HIV.

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:31.510
HIV is just wrapped up into this because

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:34.090
there's so much stigma and discrimination

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:35.750
people might not come for

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:37.630
an HIV test but for a health test

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:39.410
with all these things they'll show up.

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:41.200
And they get a gift for their baby

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.850
and for their birthing and then

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:46.600
if a woman tests positive there—they link the woman

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:49.010
to care and they make sure that she gets on

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:51.480
antiretroviral drugs, her baby receives

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.180
the treatment to stop transmission.

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.920
It's a full package to try to address the problem.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:01.380
With baby shower it’s producing some amazing results.

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:04.940
It's spray is like perfume.

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:07.140
You can spray it here and somebody else somewhere

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:10.220
hmm so what is that and comes looking

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:11.600
for what it is.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:13.810
Once they get into the village you see all that running

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:16.200
and coming oh the baby shower, I saw you today,

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:17.200
this, that, dah, dah.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:19.340
The perfume is spreading,

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:21.340
the fragrance is going everywhere.

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:28.700
Russia accounts for 80% of all the new infections

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:31.280
over the past five years in Eastern Europe

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:32.580
and Central Asia.

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:36.130
And it has about 10% new infections a year.

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:37.270
Something's really wrong.

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:38.620
Well what?

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:39.750
What's broken?

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:44.090
Well, Russia only has antiretroviral drugs reaching

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:46.880
about one third of the infected people.

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:48.400
That's a big problem.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:52.780
They do not promote needle exchange programs.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:55.020
They barely allow them.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:58.840
So the Russian epidemic has just been growing

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:02.550
at a time when it's dropping everywhere else in Europe.

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:05.650
In that harsh political environment,

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:08.569
HIV infected people and advocates

00:04:08.569 --> 00:04:10.650
have created a pharmacy—sort of an

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:12.910
underground pharmacy to supply

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:15.660
infected people with drugs when the government

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:17.259
runs out of drugs.

00:04:17.259 --> 00:04:20.150
So they collect these antiretroviral drugs

00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:22.990
and redistribute them to people in need.

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:25.469
This is for free.

00:04:25.469 --> 00:04:28.260
Just help each other.

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:32.700
When I did a just a completely unbiased look at the data,

00:04:32.719 --> 00:04:35.150
what's happening in the United States—

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:39.600
Miami has more new infections than any
city in the country.

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:42.360
That shocked me and then when I looked at

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:46.099
the top 10 cities in the country—four of
them

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:48.820
were in Florida for the new infection rate.

00:04:49.669 --> 00:04:52.819
Florida has basically every driver of the epidemic

00:04:52.819 --> 00:04:55.210
that we know of in the United States.

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:57.710
High rate of infection in men who have sex with men,

00:04:57.710 --> 00:04:59.439
it has people who inject drugs,

00:04:59.439 --> 00:05:02.180
public transportation isn't really what it should be,

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:05.069
and then our rural areas are even more challenged.

00:05:05.069 --> 00:05:08.389
Homelessness, mental health, ethnic disparities

00:05:08.389 --> 00:05:12.389
all get in the way of us dealing with the epidemic here.

00:05:12.389 --> 00:05:15.020
it's a challenge under the best of circumstances

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:17.849
to get appropriate messaging to get people into care.

00:05:17.849 --> 00:05:19.830
The Haitian community for example, a lot of

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:23.620
people don't speak English—they speak Creole.

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:29.860
So, to reach the Haitian community

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:33.129
a radio program has a health section

00:05:33.129 --> 00:05:36.240
that just goes after that Creole population.

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:40.100
A way to specifically get them HIV tested.

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:41.979
Here's a clinic in Little Haiti.

00:05:41.979 --> 00:05:43.590
It has people speaking Creole.

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:44.719
They'll pick you up at your home

00:05:44.719 --> 00:05:47.150
and take you back and forth.

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:51.270
We know if you are treated with antiretroviral drugs

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:55.080
and you drive your virus in your body down to levels

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:58.330
that are undetectable your odds of transmitting

00:05:58.330 --> 00:06:01.340
that virus to someone else are exceedingly low.

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:05.460
Myself, I take my meds all the time

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:07.960
so I have become undetectable.

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:10.800
That's at the heart of all these efforts

00:06:10.809 --> 00:06:13.009
is get everyone tested get everyone

00:06:13.009 --> 00:06:15.869
who's positive on treatment and getting everyone

00:06:15.869 --> 00:06:18.629
who's at high risk to lower their risk.

00:06:18.629 --> 00:06:20.280
To do something like needle

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:21.980
and syringe exchange programs

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:24.749
or pre-exposure prophylaxis which is a pill

00:06:24.749 --> 00:06:27.860
usually used to treat HIV that can prevent HIV.

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:29.160
That's what's needed to really break

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:30.160
the back of the epidemic.

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:33.800
And there's a whole lot we can today.

